/** @page enums Enums: Type-Safe Enumerated Constants

    @tableofcontents

    @section enum_overview Enum Overview
    The <tt><b>enum</b></tt> keyword allows for type-safe enumerated constants to be declared with a
    specific base type. Enums provide named constants that are grouped together and can be used for
    type-safe programming where only specific values are valid.

    Enum values are compatible with their underlying base type in both directions - enum constants
    can be used where the base type is expected, and base type values can be assigned to enum
    variables (with runtime validation). For guaranteed type safety, the @ref cast "cast<>" operator
    can be used to validate that a base type value corresponds to a valid enum member.

    @section enum_syntax Enum Declaration Syntax
    <tt>[<b>public</b>]</tt> <tt><b>enum</b></tt> <em>enum_identifier</em> [<tt><b>:</b></tt> <em>base_type</em>] <tt>{</tt> \n
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>member_name</em> <tt>=</tt> <em>value</em><tt>,</tt> \n
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[...] \n
    <tt>}</tt>

    The base type specifier (<tt>: <em>base_type</em></tt>) is optional. When omitted, the base type
    is inferred from the first member value. If no members have explicit values, the base type
    defaults to @ref int_type "int". All member values must be of the same type as the enum's
    base type (whether explicit or inferred).

    @par Supported Base Types
    - @ref int_type "int" - integer values (default when no base type is specified and no values are given)
    - @ref string_type "string" - string values
    - @ref float_type "float" - floating-point values
    - @ref number_type "number" - arbitrary-precision numeric values

    @par Example: Integer Enum with Explicit Base Type
    @code{.py}
public enum Priority : int {
    Low = 0,
    Medium = 5,
    High = 10,
    Critical = 100,
}

# Use enum constants
Priority p = Priority::High;
printf("Priority value: %d\n", p);  # Prints: Priority value: 10
    @endcode

    @par Example: String Enum with Explicit Base Type
    @code{.py}
public enum Status : string {
    Active = "active",
    Inactive = "inactive",
    Pending = "pending",
}

# Use enum constants
Status s = Status::Active;
printf("Status: %s\n", s);  # Prints: Status: active
    @endcode

    @par Example: Inferred Base Type
    When the base type is omitted, it is inferred from the type of the first member value:
    @code{.py}
# Base type inferred as "string" from the first member value
enum HttpMethod {
    Get = "GET",
    Post = "POST",
    Put = "PUT",
    Delete = "DELETE",
}

# Base type inferred as "int" from the first member value
enum ExitCode {
    Success = 0,
    GeneralError = 1,
    MisuseOfBuiltin = 2,
}
    @endcode

    @par Example: Member Type Mismatch (Parse Error)
    All member values must match the enum's base type:
    @code{.py}
# PARSE-EXCEPTION: enum member 'B' value type 'int' does not match enum base type 'string'
enum Bad {
    A = "hello",
    B = 42,
}
    @endcode

    @section enum_type_safety Enum Type Safety
    Enums in Qore are type-compatible with their base type in both directions. Enum values can be
    used where the base type is expected, and base type values can be assigned to enum-typed
    variables or passed to enum-typed parameters.

    @par Example: Type Compatibility
    @code{.py}
public enum Color : int {
    Red = 1,
    Green = 2,
    Blue = 3,
}

# Enum to base type - OK
int i = Color::Red;  # i = 1

# Base type to enum - OK (accepted at parse time)
int rawValue = 2;
Color c = rawValue;

# Function with enum parameter
sub processColor(Color c) {
    printf("Color value: %d\n", c);
}

processColor(Color::Blue);  # OK: enum constant
processColor(3);             # OK: base type value accepted
    @endcode

    @note While base type values are accepted where enum types are expected, using
    @ref cast "cast<enum<EnumName>>()" provides additional runtime validation that the value
    corresponds to a valid enum member. See @ref enum_cast "Casting to Enum Types" below.

    @section enum_cast Casting to Enum Types
    The @ref cast "cast<>" operator can be used to convert base type values to enum values with
    runtime validation. While base type values can be assigned directly to enum-typed variables
    (see @ref enum_type_safety "Enum Type Safety"), using a cast ensures at runtime that the value
    corresponds to a valid enum member, throwing a \c RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR if it does not.

    @par Syntax
    <tt><b>cast</b>&lt;<b>enum</b>&lt;<em>EnumName</em>&gt;&gt;(</tt><em>expression</em><tt>)</tt> \n
    <tt><b>cast</b>&lt;<b>*enum</b>&lt;<em>EnumName</em>&gt;&gt;(</tt><em>expression</em><tt>)</tt> - allows NOTHING

    @par Example: Valid Cast
    @code{.py}
public enum Status : string {
    Active = "active",
    Inactive = "inactive",
}

string s = "active";
Status st = cast<enum<Status>>(s);  # OK: "active" is a valid Status value
    @endcode

    @par Example: Invalid Cast (Runtime Error)
    @code{.py}
string s = "unknown";
Status st = cast<enum<Status>>(s);  # RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR: "unknown" is not a valid Status value
    @endcode

    @par Example: Wrong Base Type (Runtime Error)
    @code{.py}
auto v = 123;
Status st = cast<enum<Status>>(v);  # RUNTIME-CAST-ERROR: expected string value, got integer
    @endcode

    @par Example: Or-Nothing Cast
    @code{.py}
*string s;  # NOTHING
*Status st = cast<*enum<Status>>(s);  # OK: st is NOTHING

*string s2 = "active";
*Status st2 = cast<*enum<Status>>(s2);  # OK: st2 is Status::Active
    @endcode

    @section enum_namespaces Namespaced Enums
    Enums can be declared within namespaces and accessed using the namespace path.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
namespace MyApp {
    public enum LogLevel : int {
        Debug = 0,
        Info = 1,
        Warning = 2,
        Error = 3,
    }
}

# Access namespaced enum
MyApp::LogLevel level = MyApp::LogLevel::Warning;
    @endcode

    @section enum_switch Using Enums in Switch Statements
    Enum constants can be used in switch statements for pattern matching.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
public enum Status : string {
    Active = "active",
    Inactive = "inactive",
    Pending = "pending",
}

sub getStatusMessage(Status s) returns string {
    switch (s) {
        case Status::Active:
            return "The item is active";
        case Status::Inactive:
            return "The item is inactive";
        case Status::Pending:
            return "The item is pending";
    }
}
    @endcode

    @section enum_reflection Enum Reflection
    Enums can be inspected at runtime using the reflection module.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
%requires reflection

public enum Priority : int {
    Low = 1,
    Medium = 2,
    High = 3,
}

# Get enum by name
Enum e = Enum::forName("Priority");
printf("Enum name: %s\n", e.getName());
printf("Base type: %s\n", e.getBaseType().getName());

# Iterate over members
foreach EnumMember m in (e.getMembers()) {
    printf("  %s = %y\n", m.getName(), m.getValue());
}

# Validate values
printf("Is 2 valid? %y\n", e.isValidValue(2));   # True
printf("Is 99 valid? %y\n", e.isValidValue(99)); # False
    @endcode

    @section enum_type_name Direct Enum Type Names
    Enum names can be used directly as type names without the <tt>enum&lt;...&gt;</tt> wrapper.
    Both syntaxes are equivalent.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
public enum Status : string {
    Active = "active",
}

# These are equivalent:
Status s1 = Status::Active;
enum<Status> s2 = Status::Active;

# Or-nothing variants:
*Status s3;
*enum<Status> s4;

# In function parameters:
sub process(Status s) { }
sub process2(enum<Status> s) { }
    @endcode

    @section enum_restrictions Parse Option Restrictions
    Enum declarations can be disabled using the @ref Qore::PO_NO_ENUM "PO_NO_ENUM" parse option or the
    @ref no-enum "%no-enum" parse directive.

    @code{.py}
%no-enum

# The following will cause a parse error:
# public enum Status : string { Active = "active" }
    @endcode

    @see
    - @ref cast "cast<>"
    - \c Qore::Reflection::Enum "Enum class"
    - \c Qore::Reflection::EnumMember "EnumMember class"
*/
